.Dd September 12, 2026
.Dt SHMFD 9
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm shmfd ,
.Nm shmfd_init ,
.Nm shmfd_mmap ,
.Nm shmfd_close ,
.Nm shmfd_revoke
.Nd caller-owned file-mapping facade for a VM object
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/types.h
.In sys/file.h
.In sys/shmfd.h
.In vm/vm.h
.Ft int
.Fn shmfd_init "struct shmfd *shmfd" "vm_object_t object" "off_t size"
.Ft int
.Fn shmfd_mmap "struct file *fp" "vm_map_t map" "vm_offset_t *addr" "vm_size_t size" "vm_prot_t prot" "vm_prot_t maxprot" "int flags" "vm_ooffset_t foff" "struct thread *td"
.Ft void
.Fn shmfd_close "struct shmfd *shmfd"
.Ft int
.Fn shmfd_revoke "struct shmfd *shmfd"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
interface provides the VM-mapping part of a file-backed kernel export without
requiring a vnode, a cdev, or a mount point.
It is a kernel programming interface, not a user ABI.
In particular, it does not create descriptors, name objects, or implement
the POSIX shared-memory or memfd user interfaces.
.Pp
The facade is deliberately caller-owned.
The provider embeds a
.Vt struct shmfd
in an enclosing export object, owns that enclosing object's allocation and
reference/lifetime policy, and constructs the
.Vt struct file
and its
.Vt struct fileops .
The facade retains one reference to the supplied VM object and supplies only
the generic mapping, close, and device-memory revoke operations.
It never allocates or frees the enclosing object, never creates or destroys a
.Vt struct file ,
never installs descriptor operations, and never calls
.Fn fdrevoke .
.Pp
This division is intended for providers such as drivers and kernel services
which already own an object lifetime, a KLD unload reference, and any
non-mapping file operations.
It also permits the provider to embed application-specific state adjacent to
the facade rather than placing such state in the VM or VFS layers.
.Sh INITIALIZATION AND FILE COMPOSITION
Before exposing the object, the provider calls
.Fn shmfd_init .
The
.Fa object
argument is an already constructed VM object and
.Fa size
is the exported byte range.
The range must be non-negative, representable, and no larger than the object.
The provider retains its input reference; on success,
.Fn shmfd_init
takes an additional reference which is released by
.Fn shmfd_close .
.Pp
The caller must associate the facade with a file as follows:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent
.It
Set
.Fa fp->f_type
to
.Dv DTYPE_SHM .
.It
Set
.Fa fp->f_data
to the address of the embedded
.Vt struct shmfd .
.It
Use
.Fn shmfd_mmap
as the file operation's
.Fa fo_mmap
method.
.It
Provide the remaining file operations, including a close method appropriate
for the enclosing object.
.El
.Pp
The close method normally calls
.Fn shmfd_close
exactly once when the composed file object can no longer dispatch any operation
through its
.Fa f_data .
The provider chooses whether that close also permits final release of the
enclosing state.
.Pp
.Fn shmfd_close
clears the facade and releases only its retained VM-object reference.
It neither revokes mappings nor deallocates a pager, and it does not free the
enclosing storage.
It may close an unrevoked
.Dv OBJT_DEVICE
facade.
If a provider does so while a source mapping remains, it must retain the pager
handle and all state used by that pager until the VM object is torn down.
A provider which instead needs to release that state must call
.Fn shmfd_revoke
before its final release.
.Sh MAPPING
.Fn shmfd_mmap
is directly suitable for a
.Vt struct fileops
.Fa fo_mmap
method.
It validates that
.Fa fp
is a
.Dv DTYPE_SHM
file whose
.Fa f_data
is a live facade, then installs an ordinary VM mapping of the object's pager.
The caller-selected VM object determines the paging semantics; mapping is not
limited to a particular object type.
.Pp
For objects other than
.Dv OBJT_SWAP ,
the mapped range is bounded by the declared export size rounded upward to a
page boundary.
An
.Dv OBJT_SWAP
object has the ordinary POSIX shared-memory behavior: a mapping may extend
beyond the current declared size.
Access to a wholly uncovered page faults until the object is extended, while
the uncovered portion of a final partial page reads as zero.
Extending the object makes the newly covered pages available through an
existing mapping.
The offset must be page aligned.
Ordinary
.Dv MAP_SHARED
and private copy-on-write mappings are supported.
.Dv MAP_STACK
and
.Dv MAP_VPAGETABLE
are rejected.
The latter is excluded because its page-table ownership cannot be revoked
through the normal VM-object backing-list path.
.Pp
The maximum protection is derived from the file access flags and the
.Fa maxprot
limit supplied by the VM layer.
.Dv FREAD
permits read and execute mappings.
Shared writable mappings additionally require
.Dv FWRITE .
A private mapping may request write access without
.Dv FWRITE
because writes are copy-on-write and do not modify the exported object.
.Pp
As with other file mapping methods, successful mapping does not transfer
ownership of the facade or its object to the VM map.
The enclosing provider must keep the composed file object and facade valid
until their normal close path runs.
.Sh REVOCATION
.Fn shmfd_revoke
irreversibly detaches a device-memory export from its provider.
It accepts only an
.Dv OBJT_DEVICE
object.
In particular,
.Dv OBJT_SWAP ,
.Dv OBJT_MGTDEVICE ,
and other object types are not revocable through this interface.
An
.Dv OBJT_SWAP
facade is suitable for ordinary pageable user-to-user shared memory, but its
lifetime is closed with
.Fn shmfd_close ,
not with
.Fn shmfd_revoke .
.Pp
Before calling
.Fn shmfd_revoke ,
the provider must remove all of its kernel mappings of the exported device
memory and quiesce any provider activity which can create new ones.
Revocation forcibly removes user mappings; it is not a mechanism for
coordinating independent kernel consumers.
.Pp
The function serializes with new mappings, closes VM fault admission, waits for
previously admitted VM fault work and paging-in-progress activity to finish,
and invalidates the object's mappings and resident pages.
It then deallocates the device pager while the object is still
.Dv OBJT_DEVICE
and changes the object to a terminal type.
Consequently, after a successful return no later page fault can enter the
provider pager.
An existing shared mapping which is subsequently accessed faults and is
normally reported to user space as
.Dv SIGSEGV .
.Pp
Private copy-on-write pages which have already been copied into the mapping
belong to that process and remain usable.
Only an access which still requires the revoked source object faults.
Thus revocation removes access to provider backing; it does not reclaim
process-private COW data.
.Pp
Revocation affects VM mappings only.
To disable reads, writes, ioctls, or other file operations on outstanding file
descriptors, the provider may separately call
.Fn fdrevoke
with the embedded facade address and
.Dv DTYPE_SHM .
The provider chooses the credential passed to
.Fn fdrevoke ;
a revoke initiated from a prison applies only to descriptors in that prison.
Since descriptor revocation can cause the final close callback to run, possibly
freeing the enclosing object, a provider must not dereference the facade or
enclosing object after a call which may trigger that final release.
.Pp
For a loadable provider, a usual sequence is to make its outstanding-export
counter prevent unload, revoke the device-memory facade, optionally revoke its
descriptors, and let each final close drop the provider's counter.
.Fn shmfd_revoke
makes it safe for later user faults to avoid provider pager code; the provider
remains responsible for blocking KLD unload until every composed file object
and its enclosing state have completed their close path.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn shmfd_init
returns zero on success or
.Er EINVAL
for an invalid facade, object, size, terminal object, or range larger than the
object.
.Pp
.Fn shmfd_mmap
returns zero on success.
It returns
.Er EINVAL
for an invalid or closed facade, invalid flags, offset, or a range beyond a
non-swap facade's declared size, and
.Er EACCES
when the requested protection exceeds the file's permitted maximum protection.
Errors from VM-map insertion are returned using the normal
.Xr mmap 2
error mapping.
.Pp
.Fn shmfd_revoke
returns zero on success,
.Er EINVAL
for a null or closed facade,
.Er EALREADY
when the object has already entered its terminal state or fault admission has
already been closed, and
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
when the object is not
.Dv OBJT_DEVICE .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr vnode 9
